<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lazar of Serbia</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1329–<a href="/years/1389">1389</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Lazar Hrebeljanović</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lazar Hrebeljanović, known as Prince Lazar, was the most powerful Serbian ruler after the collapse of the Serbian Empire, governing Moravian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> from about 1371. Allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tvrtko-i-of-bosnia">Tvrtko I of Bosnia</a>, he defeated the rival lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikola-altomanovic">Nikola Altomanović</a> in 1373. He was killed leading the Serbian army against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kosovo">Battle of Kosovo</a> in 1389 and is venerated as a saint.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lazar-of-serbia.jpg" alt="Lazar of Serbia" width="199" height="300" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prince_Lazar_(Ravanica_Monastery).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Lazar Hrebeljanović was born about 1329 in the Fortress of Prilepac, near the mining town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novo-brdo">Novo Brdo</a>. His family were hereditary lords of Prilepac. His father Pribac was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/logothete">logothete</a>, or chancellor, at the court of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Dušan</a>, who ruled as king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> from 1331 to 1346 and as emperor from 1346 to 1355. Lazar became a stavilac at Dušan's court, an office with a role in the ceremony at the royal table. Around 1353 he married Princess Milica Nemanjić. Dušan died in 1355 and was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-uros-v">Stefan Uroš V</a>, and separatism spread as powerful nobles asserted more independence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Lazar left the court of Tsar Uroš in 1363 or 1365, aged about 35, and began his career as a regional lord, though his activity between 1363 and 1371 is poorly documented. The Mrnjavčević brothers, Vukašin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jovan-uglesa">Jovan Uglješa</a>, had become the most powerful nobles. A Ragusan document of 22 April 1371 calls him Comes Lazarus and says he held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudnik">Rudnik</a>. His state, called Moravian Serbia by historians, covered the basins of the Great, West and South Morava rivers. Between 1374 and 1379 the Serbian Church recognised him as Lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> and Podunavlje.</p><p>He sought to revive the Serbian Empire with himself at its head and claimed to be the direct successor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nemanjic-dynasty">Nemanjić dynasty</a>, which died out in 1371. The Serbian Orthodox Church supported this, but the nobility did not accept him as supreme ruler. Although he is often called Tsar Lazar, he held only the title of prince. He was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kosovo">Battle of Kosovo</a> in June 1389 while leading a Christian army against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> under Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murad-i">Murad I</a>, a battle that ended with no clear victor and heavy losses on both sides.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His widow Milica ruled as regent for their adolescent son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-lazarevic">Stefan Lazarević</a> and in 1390 accepted Ottoman suzerainty in exchange for a military alliance, to protect the weakened state from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. Lazar was first buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pristina">Priština</a> and in 1390 or 1391 his relics were moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ravanica">Ravanica Monastery</a>, which he had built. He was probably canonised then and is venerated as a martyr, with his feast day on 15 June, and was the first lay person recognised as a saint after the Nemanjić rulers. In epic poetry the night before the battle he chose a heavenly kingdom over an earthly one. Monks took his relics north in 1690 during the Great Serb Migration.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lazar of Serbia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Moravian Serbia under <b>Lazar Hrebeljanović</b> became the strongest successor and fought the Ottomans at the Battle of Kosovo in 1389.</blockquote><a href="/empire/serbian-empire">Serbian Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Vuk Branković, son-in-law of <b>Prince Lazar</b>, built up lands in Kosovo after the collapse of the Serbian Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/district-of-brankovic">District of Branković</a></li><li><blockquote>He was aggressive towards his neighbours, plotting against <b>Prince Lazar</b> and Tvrtko I of the Banate of Bosnia.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-nikola-altomanovic">Lordship of Nikola Altomanović</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Serbian Empire broke up, Prince <b>Lazar Hrebeljanović</b>, a court official under Dušan, built the strongest of the successor states in the Morava valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/moravian-serbia">Moravian Serbia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Prilepac, in what is now <a href="/countries/kosovo">Kosovo</a>. Died in Kosovo Field, in what is now <a href="/countries/kosovo">Kosovo</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.54,21.52&amp;year=1368" aria-label="Prilepac on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Lazar of Serbia"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4468 3916h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4468 3916h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.54,21.52&amp;year=1368">What was here in 1368? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
